#X obj 690 8 replace;
#X obj 230 78 cnv 1 535 337 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -237178 -1 0;
#X text 240 83 Replaces atoms in the incoming data stream: every atom
equal to the "from" value is replaced by the "to" value. Both values
can be specified either as the @from and @to properties \, or as the
first and second arguments.;
#X text 240 153 Typical use cases: renaming symbols or note names in
a stream (for example \, translating note names \, remapping parameter
names \, or normalizing case) \, replacing placeholder values such
as 0 or -1 with meaningful defaults \, and remapping small sets of
numeric codes — for example \, converting controller numbers \, scale
degrees \, or routing tags from one convention to another.;
#X text 240 269 The object is especially useful in data cleanup tasks
— when incoming data uses a different naming convention \, contains
placeholder values that need to be interpreted \, or needs to be adapted
to the requirements of a downstream object. Unlike [select] + [message]
chains \, [replace] handles many substitutions at once inside a single
data stream \, without splitting and rebuilding lists manually.;
